```mermaid
flowchart TB
    Start([Problem / team constraints]) --> Q1{Team size<br/>< 15 engineers?}
    Q1 -->|yes| Q2{Latency-critical<br/>steady load?}
    Q1 -->|no, 15+| MS[Microservices]
    Q2 -->|yes| Mono[Modular Monolith]
    Q2 -->|no, bursty or idle| Q3{Event-driven<br/>irregular traffic?}
    Q3 -->|yes| Serverless[Serverless / FaaS]
    Q3 -->|no| Mono
    MS --> Q4{Traffic volume<br/>extremely bursty?}
    Q4 -->|yes, parts of it| Hybrid[Microservices + Serverless<br/>mixed]
    Q4 -->|no, predictable| PureMS[Microservices only]
```

**3+ independent squads** with different release cadences. If the UI team releases weekly and the data team monthly, extracting those into separate deployables frees each from the other's schedule.

**SLO differentiation between components.** The checkout flow needs 99.99% uptime; the analytics dashboard is fine at 99.5%. Extracting checkout as its own service lets you overprovision just for it.

**Polyglot needs.** The ML team wants Python; the recommendations team wants Go; the core is Java. Microservices let each squad pick their tool without a frankenstein monolith.

**Scaling differentiation.** The image-processing service needs 50 GPU-enabled nodes; the API tier needs 200 cheap replicas. Separate services, separate resource profiles.

**Independent data models.** When bounded contexts genuinely want different data stores (orders in Postgres, search in Elasticsearch, cache in Redis as primary store), data-per-service fits.

**Operational maturity exists.** CI/CD is solid, observability (traces, metrics, logs) is instrumented, on-call culture is mature. Without these, microservices amplify chaos.

**Example: Spring Boot microservice with standard cloud stack:**

```yaml
# application.yml
spring:
  application.name: order-service
  cloud:
    discovery.client.simple.enabled: false      # use k8s native
    config.import: configserver:http://config-server:8888

server:
  port: 8080

management:
  endpoints.web.exposure.include: health,info,prometheus
  tracing.sampling.probability: 0.1
  otlp.tracing.endpoint: http://tempo:4318/v1/traces

resilience4j:
  circuitbreaker.instances.payment-svc:
    failureRateThreshold: 50
    slidingWindowSize: 20
```

```java
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableFeignClients
public class OrderServiceApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) { SpringApplication.run(OrderServiceApp.class, args); }
}

@FeignClient(name = "payment-svc")
interface PaymentClient {
    @PostMapping("/charge")
    ChargeResponse charge(@RequestBody ChargeRequest req);
}

@Service
class OrderService {
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "payment-svc", fallbackMethod = "queueForLater")
    public Order placeOrder(Order o) {
        paymentClient.charge(new ChargeRequest(o));
        return orderRepo.save(o);
    }
}
```

Alongside: Kubernetes manifests, Helm chart, Argo CD config, Prometheus scrape, Loki log source, Tempo trace source, Grafana dashboards. That's the operational minimum — not optional.

**Order service publishes event on creation:**

```java
@Service
@Transactional
class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepo repo;
    private final OutboxRepo outboxRepo;

    public Order placeOrder(OrderRequest req) {
        Order order = repo.save(Order.newPending(req));
        outboxRepo.save(OutboxEvent.of("OrderCreated", new OrderCreatedEvent(order)));
        return order;
    }
}
```

**Inventory service subscribes:**

```java
@Component
class InventoryReservationListener {
    private final InventoryService inventory;
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, Object> kafka;

    @KafkaListener(topics = "orders", groupId = "inventory-reservation")
    public void on(OrderCreatedEvent e) {
        try {
            inventory.reserve(e.getOrderId(), e.getItems());
            kafka.send("inventory", new StockReservedEvent(e.getOrderId()));
        } catch (InsufficientStockException ex) {
            kafka.send("inventory", new StockReservationFailedEvent(e.getOrderId(), ex.getMessage()));
        }
    }
}
```

**Payment service subscribes to StockReserved:**

```java
@Component
class PaymentListener {
    @KafkaListener(topics = "inventory", groupId = "payment-saga")
    public void on(StockReservedEvent e) {
        try {
            paymentService.authorize(e.getOrderId());
            kafka.send("payments", new PaymentAuthorizedEvent(e.getOrderId()));
        } catch (PaymentException ex) {
            kafka.send("payments", new PaymentFailedEvent(e.getOrderId(), ex.getMessage()));
            // inventory will need to release — see compensating listener below
        }
    }
}
```

**Compensating listener in inventory service:**

```java
@Component
class InventoryCompensationListener {
    @KafkaListener(topics = "payments", groupId = "inventory-compensation")
    public void on(PaymentFailedEvent e) {
        inventory.release(e.getOrderId());   // idempotent release
        kafka.send("inventory", new StockReleasedEvent(e.getOrderId()));
    }
}
```

**Where choreography hurts:**
- The overall flow lives *nowhere* — you reconstruct it from listener code across repos
- Adding shipping service requires editing order, inventory, payment listener configs + new compensations
- Debugging 'why did order 42 end in StockReleased state' traces through 5 services' logs
- Cyclic event storms are possible: service A emits X → B reacts Y → A reacts Y → B reacts X (rare but ugly)

For 2–3 services and simple flows, choreography is the leanest option. Past 4–5 services with compensations, it becomes distributed spaghetti.

**Temporal workflow (Java SDK) — flow is readable top-to-bottom:**

```java
@WorkflowInterface
public interface OrderSagaWorkflow {
    @WorkflowMethod
    OrderResult placeOrder(OrderRequest req);
}

public class OrderSagaWorkflowImpl implements OrderSagaWorkflow {

    private final ActivityOptions opts = ActivityOptions.newBuilder()
        .setStartToCloseTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
        .setRetryOptions(RetryOptions.newBuilder().setMaximumAttempts(3).build())
        .build();

    private final InventoryActivities inventory = Workflow.newActivityStub(InventoryActivities.class, opts);
    private final PaymentActivities   payment   = Workflow.newActivityStub(PaymentActivities.class, opts);
    private final ShippingActivities  shipping  = Workflow.newActivityStub(ShippingActivities.class, opts);

    @Override
    public OrderResult placeOrder(OrderRequest req) {
        Saga saga = new Saga(new Saga.Options.Builder().build());
        try {
            ReservationId resId = inventory.reserve(req.items());
            saga.addCompensation(() -> inventory.release(resId));

            PaymentId payId = payment.authorize(req.customerId(), req.total());
            saga.addCompensation(() -> payment.refund(payId));

            ShipmentId shipId = shipping.schedule(req);
            saga.addCompensation(() -> shipping.cancel(shipId));

            return new OrderResult(req.orderId(), resId, payId, shipId, SUCCESS);
        } catch (ActivityFailure e) {
            saga.compensate();
            throw Workflow.wrap(e);
        }
    }
}
```

**Activity interfaces are thin HTTP / gRPC calls per service:**

```java
@ActivityInterface
public interface InventoryActivities {
    ReservationId reserve(List<Item> items);
    void release(ReservationId id);
}
```

**The flow is readable in one place.** Compensation is explicit — `saga.addCompensation(...)`. Retries are declared on ActivityOptions. Timeouts per step. Temporal's runtime persists every state change; crashes resume from the last checkpoint.

**Orchestrator benefits:**
- **Visible flow**: Temporal UI shows running workflows, their current step, history
- **Explicit compensation**: no guessing what happens on failure
- **Durable execution**: orchestrator crashes don't lose state; workflows resume
- **Versioning**: change flow logic with explicit migration patterns
- **Testing**: unit-test the workflow logic without live services

**Orchestrator costs:**
- Running a Temporal cluster (or Camunda, Conductor) — extra infrastructure
- Vendor-specific workflow DSL (less portable)
- Learning curve (workflow determinism, activity vs workflow distinction)
- Central component failure affects all workflows (though Temporal is designed for HA)

**Camunda / BPMN alternative:** visual workflow designed by business analysts, executed by engine; powerful for regulated industries (insurance claims, loans) where the flow is a compliance artifact. Heavier but visual.

**AWS Step Functions** for AWS-native: JSON ASL, integrates with every AWS service, visible in console. Cheaper ops than Temporal but more AWS lock-in.
